Right — and the important part is the apply it.

Materials science becomes useful when you can run the full chain:

composition + structure → processing → microstructure → properties → performance in an actual environment.

Then reverse it when designing: desired behavior → required properties → candidate structures/materials → manufacturing route → tests → observed failure/delta → redesign.

That logic travels well beyond literal metals, polymers, ceramics, and composites. Once you understand things like interfaces, defects, fatigue, phase transitions, anisotropy, gradients, fracture, diffusion, sacrificial layers, and composite architectures, they become reusable models for designing other complex systems too—provided we keep the analogy separate from the actual physics.

So you don’t merely ask, “What is this made of?” You ask, “Why is it arranged this way, what loads and environment shaped that arrangement, where will it fail, and what change gives me the property I want?” That’s the transferable operator.